{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And he made the court of the south side, southward.</b> He gave precedence to the south wind, which is called 'right,' because it is to the right of one entering the court." "<b>And there are</b> some among the scholars of astronomy who say that the south is the head and beginning of the other winds, and the north is the end and tail of the winds; therefore Reuben, who was the firstborn, was placed in the south, and Dan at the end of the north." "<b>And know</b> that the account of the matter of the Tabernacle and its vessels, and the investigation of the arrangement of its forms and its openings, and the measure of their length and breadth and height — even though the Temple is not standing — is a great mitzvah, whose reward reaches to the heavens, and it is Torah, and we are required to learn it. And so the Rabbis, of blessed memory, said concerning the matter of the sacrifices: whoever engages in studying the sections and reasons out their matters in his heart, it is as though he offered the sacrifice itself. That is what they said: whoever engages in the section of the עולה — it is as though he offered an עולה; in the section of the חטאת — it is as though he offered a חטאת; and so with all of them. From this you learn concerning reciting the matter by mouth alone — his reward is with him and his deed is accounted before Him as though he performed the act and offered a sacrifice on the altar. And the same applies to the account of the matters of the Tabernacle and the Temple: we have great merit and abundant recompense when we engage with them and strive to understand their plain sense and their revealed aspects — how much more so if we merit to grasp their hidden content, to comprehend one of their allusions. And this is what David said concerning this matter, concerning the hidden and the revealed (Psalms 48:13–14): ״סבו ציון והקיפוה ספרו מגדליה שיתו לבכם לחילה פסגו ארמנותיה למען תספרו לדור אחרון״; and by the merit of the encircling and the compassing, in the revealed and in the hidden, He will restore His Shekhinah into it, as it says (ibid.): ״כי זה אלהים אלהינו עולם ועד הוא ינהגנו על מות״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {2 "The phrase אע\"פ שאין בהמ\"ק קיים reads literally 'even though the Temple (בית המקדש) is not standing'; the abbreviation בהמ\"ק resolved as בית המקדש. The final word מות is translated hyper-literally as written; the psalm verse may read על מות in some traditions (others מות as 'death' or emended to מעלה); difficulty recorded, not smoothed."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:07:48.885582071Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}